{"qid":"Q5406","names":["Hittites"],"title":"Hittites","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Hittites were an Anatolian Indo-European people who built one of the Bronze Age Near East's major powers, centred on their capital Hattusa in north-central Anatolia from roughly 1650 BC. Under Suppiluliuma I (c. 1344–1322 BC) and his successors they dominated Syria, rivalled Egypt, and forced Ramesses II to conclude the Treaty of Kadesh (c. 1259 BC), the oldest surviving peace treaty. The empire collapsed abruptly around 1180 BC amid the broader Late Bronze Age crisis, with Hattusa burned and abandoned; a residual string of Neo-Hittite city-states in Syria persisted until the Assyrian conquests of the 8th century BC.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Hittite territorial extent is well-attested through a large cuneiform archive at Hattusa (Bogazköy), diplomatic correspondence with Egypt and Assyria, and treaty texts naming vassal states; core Anatolia is solidly documented, while peripheral frontiers in Syria and the southeast are reconstructed from campaign annals and remain approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Hittites","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hittites"},{"label":"Britannica: Hittite","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Hittite"}],"britannica":"topic/Hittite","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Hittites","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Pithana of Kussara","nativeName":"𒁉𒋫𒄩𒈾","roman":"Pithana","start":-1800,"end":-1780,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Anitta of Kussara","nativeName":"𒀭𒀭𒀭𒀀𒉌𒀀𒋫","roman":"Anitta","start":-1780,"end":-1750,"image":null,"imageU